<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Guwahati</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Guwahati is the largest city of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/assam">Assam</a>, in north-eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a>, on the south bank of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brahmaputra">Brahmaputra</a>. In ancient times, as Pragjyotishpura, it was a capital of the kingdom of Kamarupa, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kamakhya-temple">Kamakhya</a> temple on Nilachal hill is a major <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hinduism">Hindu</a> pilgrimage site. Contested between the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ahom-people">Ahoms</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mughal-empire">Mughals</a> in the 17th century, it is now the main commercial centre of the region, with the state capital at Dispur within it.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/guwahati.jpg" alt="Guwahati" width="320" height="240" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Rituraj Bhuyan from Guwahati, India · CC BY-SA 2.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Guwahati_citysky.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The Ahoms retook <b>Guwahati</b> in 1667, and in 1671 the general Lachit Borphukan defeated a Mughal force under Raja Ram Singh I of Amber at the Battle of Saraighat, fought largely on the river.</blockquote><a href="/empire/ahom-kingdom">Ahom kingdom · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>…as early literature in each: Luipa was from Kamarupa, and Sarahapa is said to have come from Rani near present-day <b>Guwahati</b>; Rahul Sankrityayan held that most of the poets were from Bihar and wrote an early Magahi, and K. P. Jayaswal took the language for an early Maithili.</blockquote><a href="/songs/charyapada">Charyapada</a></li><li><blockquote>In January 1946 at <b>Guwahati</b>, Gandhi urged that the new greeting &quot;Jai Hind&quot; should not replace Vande Mataram.</blockquote><a href="/songs/vande-mataram">Vande Mataram</a></li><li><blockquote>Koch Hajo was a Hindu kingdom whose rulers patronised the Hayagriva Madhava temple at Hajo and the Kamakhya temple near <b>Guwahati</b>, which the Koch had rebuilt.</blockquote><a href="/state/koch-hajo">Koch Hajo</a></li></ul></section>
